+For each unbounded positive input n, both B and its unknown root A belong to the same Z/nZ. The operation uses all pairs, including diagonal pairs. Sparse input specifies binary n and sorted distinct canonical binary residues; dense input specifies binary n and exactly n incidence bits. Invalid encodings are rejected in polynomial time. The theorem is deterministic polynomial many-one NP-completeness separately for each language, already when n is odd, B is nonempty, contains0, and every canonical2b<n. Deterministic polynomial exact decision exists iff P=NP; this does not assert P≠NP.
